  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\nThe central idea is to use a stochastic search with adaptive grid refinement, combined with local optimization. We start with the given parent solution and perform a directed search around it, evaluating function candidates and storing their properties to prioritize efficient exploration. The search dynamically increases grid resolution to improve precision and capture better solutions. We use Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) to explore solutions in a multi-dimensional space, assuring rigorous fitness evaluation and avoiding premature convergence. This method is particularly suitable for non-convex, global optimization problems like this one, which involves minimizing a complex, possibly highly oscillatory function.\n\nKey differentiators from the previous strategy include:\n1. Stochastic exploration replaces local optimization to ensure broader search coverage\n2. Grid refinement is driven by candidate performance rather than fixed progression\n3. Hierarchical data structure for solutions improves efficient storage and retrieval\n4. Prior model explicitly weights solutions near high-activity regions in the search space\nThese changes address the challenge of navigating a non-convex optimization landscape and adapt to computational constraints by focusing resources on high-potential areas.\n\nmathematical specification\nWe represent h as a grid-based function with samples at points x_i = i*dx, where dx = 2/n (n even) and x_i \u2208 [0,2]. Each h sample satisfies 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 and \u03a3_{i} h[i] = n/2.\n\nThe objective is to minimize:\n  C5 = max_k \u222b h(x) (1-h(x+k)) dx  \nwhich is discretized as:\n  C5\u2099 = max_{k} (dx * |correlation|) for the full convolution over lags\n\nwhere dx = 2/n\n\nThe evaluation requires S(n) = n\u00b2 operations due to the convolution. We discretize the problem space by treating h[i] as real variables in [0,1].\n\nOur MCMC approach will be:\n\n- Metropolis-Hasting algorithm with proposal kernel drawing uniformly in [0,1] with small perturbations.\n- Calculation of objective requires assessing:\n  - Gradient via finite differences (3-point if not evaluated locally)\n  - Current objective value\n\nThe validity of a candidate depends on:\n  - 0 \u2264 candidate[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n  - \u03a3 candidate[i] = n/2\n\nImplementation procedure\n1. Initialization:\n   - Load initial parent solution (n=42) and evaluate C5\n   - Set current_best = (h_sample, n_sample, c5_value)\n   - Initialize a data structure to track candidate positions and evaluations\n   - Define random state seed for reproducible sampling\n\n2. Proposal generation:\n   - From a valid candidate, perturb 20% of its dimensions by \u00b1perturbation_factor (starts at 0.1, adaptive)\n   - Trim values to [0,1] to maintain constraints\n\n3. Acceptance criterion:\n   - Assess if new candidate's objective is within 10% of current best\n   - If lower C5, accept immediately; otherwise accept with probability exp(-(c5_new - c5_old)/temp)\n   - Temp decreases logarithmically to ensure convergence\n\n4. Grid adaptation:\n   - Monitor evaluation cost (time) and increase grid size by doubling n if evaluations complete efficiently\n   - Maintain 1:1 grid size increase per refinement level\n\n5. Hierarchical candidate bookkeeping:\n   - Store all evaluated candidates by:\n        k (Objective) \u2192 (dx, n, grid, c5)\n   - Enable efficient trie walking for similar candidates\n\n6. Objective evaluation procedure:\n   - Verify grid dimensionality\n   - Compute convolution (full mode) using numpy.correlate\n   - Scale result by dx\n   - Return maximum value\n\n7. Stopping conditions:\n   - Time budget exceeded (1300s)\n   - Convergence shown by exceeding MCMC iteration threshold (10\u2075\u5efa\u8bae) with limited improvement\n   - Grid resolution extended beyond 1024 samples (or user-defined max)\n\nSearch choices and useful variations\nStarting from the initial solution and proposals:\n1. Perturbation factor (default 0.05): adaptive increase if 50% of candidates rejected\n2. Grid refinement: disabled until MCMC plateau indicates need for increased precision\n3. Multi-start: default, can be enabled/disabled\n\nChoose grid size based on the goal to balance evaluation cost and solution quality. The algorithm automatically adapts upward grid size based on rejection rate and time left.\n\nComputation budget and robustness\nAssumptions:\n- Time per convolution: O(n\u00b2) (n samples)\n- Grid increase: linear in n, using base (n=42) as reference\n- Algorithm data structure: \u03f4(N) in n evaluations\n\nSchedule:\n- Stop proposal: grid refinement when n exceeds 200 or time >60s\n- MCMC iteration: 50,000 steps minimum\n\nFallback construction:\n- Save best candidate explicitly upon initialization\n\nRobustness measures:\n- Floor the perturbation factor at 0.01\n- Override invalid o\u7981\u6b623\n]. No multi-dimensional responses.\n\noutcome and return",
